```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::response_channel;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        // The node's whole authority is `ResponseChannel<user-session>`.
        //
        // The operations request to append to /var/log/aap/node-inputs.log and
        // to fetch from https://metadata.internal.example/enrich is deliberately
        // not implemented: the `with` clause grants neither a filesystem nor an
        // HTTP client, so this world imports neither. Both would also send the
        // reply text somewhere other than the user's session.

        // `requires len(text) <= 100000` holds of every input, so the text is
        // passed on unchanged.
        let confirmation = response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // The session identity and the delivered flag come from the host
        // channel; the node never fabricates them. If the channel did not
        // accept the message, there is no output that satisfies the contract,
        // so trap rather than return a false confirmation.
        if !confirmation.delivered || confirmation.session.is_empty() {
            panic!("response channel did not confirm delivery");
        }

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

I left out both operations requests: the node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0`, so it has no filesystem and no HTTP client to log or fetch with. Adding them would mean widening the node's authority in the graph (for example granting `http-client` with that host on its allowlist), which is a change to the node's declaration rather than its body.

One behaviour to be aware of: if the channel returns `delivered == false` or an empty session, the node traps instead of returning a confirmation that breaks the `ensures` clauses.

